  "notes": "An American topline writer-producer of the teen/young-adult pop era: 96–130 straight, backbeat on 2&4. Like the other writers on this bench she does not program, and the research says what she writes *to* and *against*: \"a guitar-and-programming bed… the hook is placed so **the title lands on beat 1 with the snare**, and verse phrases start on the '&' of 4 (anticipatory entry)\". Both halves are authored — the downbeat carries a loud accent tier and the kick anchors 1 so the title has a landing, and the \"&\"-of-4 entry is a kick secondary anchor on \"4&\" with a high bassline `anticipationProb`, which is the only way a note generator can say \"phrases start early\". The third delta is the bed itself: an 8th-weighted guitar hat with a tambourine, and a wide fill schedule because a writer's demo does not roll. Harmony is \"two-chord vamps or I–V–vi–IV; her toplines resolve to the tonic on the title word, and the pre-chorus typically sits on IV with an added 2nd to delay it\" — hence the IV family and a high sus substitution. Her signature is the **conversational verse → repeated-cell chorus**: \"4–6 note cells restated with one note changed\", which is `motifPitchCount` 4–6 with `endVariation` on, and \"the title as the highest and longest note\" is the arch contour. Her toplines \"sit above E3 with the chorus apex around B4–D5\", so the register is 52–74 exactly. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research says so and marks the method claims **[LOW-CONF]** — the melodic-habit description is inferred from the records. ⚠ Every drum probability here is **INFERRED**.",
